WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.290
- All right, now let's
work through this together.

00:00:02.290 --> 00:00:04.230
And we can see that all of the choices

00:00:04.230 --> 00:00:07.200
are expressed as a
polynomial in factored form.

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:09.430
And factored form is
useful when we're thinking

00:00:09.430 --> 00:00:11.070
about the roots of a polynomial,

00:00:11.070 --> 00:00:15.260
the x-values that make that
polynomial equal to zero.

00:00:15.260 --> 00:00:18.650
The roots are also evident when
we look at this graph here.

00:00:18.650 --> 00:00:20.560
We have a root at x equals -4,

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:24.130
a root at x equals -1.5, or -3/2,

00:00:24.130 --> 00:00:26.890
and a root at x is equal to 1.

00:00:26.890 --> 00:00:28.527
So really what we have to do is say,

00:00:28.527 --> 00:00:31.200
"Which of these factors are consistent

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:32.680
with the roots that we see?"

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:34.610
So let's go root by root.

00:00:34.610 --> 00:00:39.050
So here on the left we
have a root at x equals -4.

00:00:39.050 --> 00:00:41.760
In order for this polynomial to be zero

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:46.170
when x is equal to -4,
that means that x + 4

00:00:46.170 --> 00:00:48.900
must be a factor, or some multiple,

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:51.510
or some constant times x + 4,

00:00:51.510 --> 00:00:54.970
must be a factor of our polynomial.

00:00:54.970 --> 00:00:56.710
Now we can see in the choices that

00:00:56.710 --> 00:00:58.920
we have a bunch of x + 4s,

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:01.020
but they have different exponents on them.

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:05.170
The first one has a 2 as an
exponent, it's being squared,

00:01:05.170 --> 00:01:08.620
while the others have a 1 as the exponent.

00:01:08.620 --> 00:01:10.380
Now what we've talked
about in other videos,

00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:12.540
when we talked about multiplicity,

00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:15.190
we said, "Hey, if we see a sign change

00:01:15.190 --> 00:01:17.770
around a root, like we're
seeing right over here

00:01:17.770 --> 00:01:19.610
around x equals -4,"

00:01:19.610 --> 00:01:21.100
that means that we are going to see

00:01:21.100 --> 00:01:25.250
an odd exponent on the
corresponding factor.

00:01:25.250 --> 00:01:26.810
But if we didn't see a sign change,

00:01:26.810 --> 00:01:28.270
as we see in this other root over here,

00:01:28.270 --> 00:01:31.240
that means we would see an even exponent.

00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:33.300
Now, we clearly see the sign change,

00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:34.860
so we would expect an odd exponent,

00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:37.630
and, of course, 1 is an
odd number and 2 isn't.

00:01:37.630 --> 00:01:39.900
So if you just have a straight up x + 4,

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:43.300
you would have a sign
change around x equals -4.

00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:46.570
So I can rule out this first choice,

00:01:46.570 --> 00:01:49.027
these other three choices
are still looking good

00:01:50.070 --> 00:01:52.460
based on just the x + 4 factor.

00:01:52.460 --> 00:01:56.230
Now lets move on to the
next factor right over here,

00:01:56.230 --> 00:01:57.470
so, or the next root.

00:01:57.470 --> 00:02:02.340
The next root is at x is equal to -3/2,

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:06.290
and so one way to think
about it is you could have

00:02:06.290 --> 00:02:10.800
a factor that looks like x + 3/2,

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:13.340
or this times some constant.

00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:16.010
Now, when we look at the choices,
or the remaining choices,

00:02:16.010 --> 00:02:18.980
we don't see x + 3/2,
but we do see something

00:02:18.980 --> 00:02:21.800
that involves a 2 and a 3, and
so one way to think about it

00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:24.942
is just, "Hey, if I just
multiply this by the constant 2,

00:02:24.942 --> 00:02:29.020
that would get us 2x +
3," well I do see that

00:02:29.020 --> 00:02:31.100
right over here, and
then the next question is

00:02:31.100 --> 00:02:32.810
what should be the exponent?

00:02:32.810 --> 00:02:35.190
Well, once again we have a sign change

00:02:35.190 --> 00:02:38.850
around x equals -3/2, so we would expect

00:02:38.850 --> 00:02:40.820
an odd exponent there.

00:02:40.820 --> 00:02:42.750
And you can see out of the choices,

00:02:42.750 --> 00:02:44.880
only two of them have an exponent of 1,

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:46.270
which is an odd number,
while the other one

00:02:46.270 --> 00:02:48.720
has an even exponent there, so we can rule

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:50.260
that one out as well.

00:02:50.260 --> 00:02:52.370
And then we go to this last root.

00:02:52.370 --> 00:02:54.310
Ah, we'll do this in an orange color.

00:02:54.310 --> 00:02:56.500
We have a root at x equals 1,

00:02:56.500 --> 00:02:59.710
so we would expect x - 1, or this

00:02:59.710 --> 00:03:03.290
multiplied by some constant,
to be one of the factors.

00:03:03.290 --> 00:03:06.540
And what's interesting here
is we don't see a sign change

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:10.680
around x equals 1, so we
would expect an even exponent.

00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:12.980
And so, out of the remaining choices,

00:03:12.980 --> 00:03:16.809
we see an x - 1 in both of them,

00:03:16.809 --> 00:03:20.120
but only choice C has the even exponent

00:03:20.120 --> 00:03:25.120
that we would expect, so
choice C is looking good.

00:03:25.690 --> 00:03:27.490
If we were to look at choice D,

00:03:27.490 --> 00:03:29.140
where this is to the first power,

00:03:29.140 --> 00:03:30.970
we would expect a sign change

00:03:30.970 --> 00:03:33.480
around x is equal to 1, so this would be

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:36.460
a situation where the curve
would keep going down,

00:03:36.460 --> 00:03:39.050
something like that, so we like choice C.

00:03:39.050 --> 00:03:41.790
Let's do another example.

00:03:41.790 --> 00:03:43.207
So, once again, we are asked,

00:03:43.207 --> 00:03:44.600
"What could be the equation of p?"

00:03:44.600 --> 00:03:46.050
and we're given a graph, so again

00:03:46.050 --> 00:03:48.690
pause this video and try
to work through that.

00:03:48.690 --> 00:03:50.940
All right, we're going
to do the same idea.

00:03:50.940 --> 00:03:53.220
Let's go to this first
root right over here.

00:03:53.220 --> 00:03:56.300
We have a root at x equals -3,

00:03:56.300 --> 00:03:58.780
so we would expect some multiple

00:03:58.780 --> 00:04:02.970
of x + 3 to be one of the factors.

00:04:02.970 --> 00:04:06.910
We also have a sign
change around x equals -3,

00:04:06.910 --> 00:04:11.100
so we would expect an odd multiplicity

00:04:11.100 --> 00:04:13.500
and we would expect an odd exponent

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:15.420
on the x + 3 factor.

00:04:15.420 --> 00:04:17.020
When we look at all the choices,

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:18.833
C and D have an even exponent,

00:04:18.833 --> 00:04:20.820
so if we had x + 3 to the fourth,

00:04:20.820 --> 00:04:22.170
then you wouldn't have a sign change here,

00:04:22.170 --> 00:04:23.940
you would just touch the x-axis

00:04:23.940 --> 00:04:26.040
and then go back to
where it was coming from.

00:04:26.040 --> 00:04:28.110
So we can rule out these choices.

00:04:28.110 --> 00:04:30.805
And now, let's look at the second root.

00:04:30.805 --> 00:04:34.710
Right over here, at x is equal to 2,

00:04:34.710 --> 00:04:38.090
so we would expect x - 2
to be one of the factors,

00:04:38.090 --> 00:04:40.500
or a multiple of this,
and because we don't have

00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:42.750
a sign change around x equals 2,

00:04:42.750 --> 00:04:44.590
the graph just touches the x-axis

00:04:44.590 --> 00:04:46.490
and goes back to where it was coming from,

00:04:46.490 --> 00:04:48.950
we would expect an even exponent here.

00:04:48.950 --> 00:04:52.700
And so when we look at the
choices around the x -2 factor,

00:04:52.700 --> 00:04:55.190
we see only one of them
has an even exponent,

00:04:55.190 --> 00:04:58.250
so I am liking Choice B,

00:04:58.250 --> 00:04:59.433
and we are done.

